type Chunk = { id: string; text: string; updatedAt: string };
type Citation = { sourceId: string; quote: string };
type Draft = { answer: string; citations: Citation[] };

export async function answerPolicy(question: string) {
  const chunks = await retrieve(question, { k: 6 });

  const draft = (await callModel({
    schema: DRAFT_SCHEMA,
    temperature: 0,
    messages: [
      { role: "system", content: POLICY_PROMPT }, // "Answer only from the context. Cite every claim."
      { role: "user", content: `${formatChunks(chunks)}\n\nQuestion: ${question}` },
    ],
  })) as Draft;

  const retrieved = new Set(chunks.map((c) => c.id));
  const grounded = draft.citations.every((c) => retrieved.has(c.sourceId));

  if (!grounded) {
    return { status: "needs-review" as const, answer: draft.answer, sources: [] };
  }
  return { status: "grounded" as const, answer: draft.answer, sources: draft.citations };
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Two predictions, both from the same line.

**Case 1 — no citations.** If the model returns `citations: []`, then `draft.citations.every(...)` evaluates on an empty array, and `Array.prototype.every` returns `true` when there is nothing to test. `retrieved.has` is never called. The function returns `{ status: "grounded", answer, sources: [] }`, so an answer with zero evidence is labelled grounded and the prose is rendered. This is the vacuous-truth bug: the safest-looking branch is taken precisely when the model gave you nothing to check.

**Case 2 — a real but unsupported citation.** The check asks only whether `sourceId` is a member of the retrieved set. It never looks at `chunk.text`, and it never looks at `citation.quote`. So the model can attach `{ sourceId: "policy-refunds", quote: "..." }` to a sentence the chunk does not contain and still pass: the id exists, therefore the claim is "grounded". Membership proves the document was in context; it does not prove the sentence is supported by it. That is the difference between a citation and evidence, and it is the failure QA found.

The rest of the defects:

1. **The unit of verification is the answer, not the claim.** `answer` is one blob and `citations` is a flat list, with no mapping from claim to citation, so even a correct support check would have nothing to check against. Claim-level structure is the prerequisite for every other mitigation.
2. **No abstention state.** When `chunks` is empty or all six are irrelevant, the model still receives a prompt that demands an answer, and there is no `not-enough-evidence` branch. The system prompt asking it to "answer only from the context" is a request, not a guarantee.
3. **The unverified answer leaks anyway.** `needs-review` still returns `answer` and drops the sources, so a UI that renders by status can show unverified prose, and a human reviewer cannot see what the model cited in order to reject it.
4. **`temperature: 0` is doing no work here.** It removes most of the sampling randomness, which makes an eval more repeatable, but an unsupported fluent sentence is still the most likely continuation. Grounding is an evidence problem, not a randomness problem.
5. **No provenance.** `updatedAt` is retrieved and then thrown away, so a stale-but-cited policy is indistinguishable from a current one.

Refactor to claim-level units with a real support check and an explicit abstention path:

```ts
type Chunk = { id: string; text: string; updatedAt: string };
type Claim = { text: string; sourceId: string; quote: string };
type Draft = { claims: Claim[] };

const normalize = (s: string) => s.replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim().toLowerCase();

type ClaimCheck =
  | { ok: true; claim: Claim; source: { id: string; updatedAt: string } }
  | { ok: false; claim: Claim; reason: "unknown-source" | "quote-not-in-source" };

function checkClaim(claim: Claim, byId: Map<string, Chunk>): ClaimCheck {
  const chunk = byId.get(claim.sourceId);
  if (!chunk) return { ok: false, claim, reason: "unknown-source" };
  if (!normalize(chunk.text).includes(normalize(claim.quote))) {
    return { ok: false, claim, reason: "quote-not-in-source" };
  }
  return { ok: true, claim, source: { id: chunk.id, updatedAt: chunk.updatedAt } };
}

export async function answerPolicy(question: string) {
  const chunks = await retrieve(question, { k: 6 });
  if (chunks.length === 0) return { status: "not-enough-evidence" as const };

  const draft = (await callModel({
    schema: DRAFT_SCHEMA, // { claims: [{ text, sourceId, quote }] }
    temperature: 0,
    messages: [
      { role: "system", content: POLICY_PROMPT },
      { role: "user", content: `${formatChunks(chunks)}\n\nQuestion: ${question}` },
    ],
  })) as Draft;

  const byId = new Map(chunks.map((c) => [c.id, c]));
  const checked = draft.claims.map((claim) => checkClaim(claim, byId));
  const supported = checked.filter((c): c is Extract<ClaimCheck, { ok: true }> => c.ok);
  const rejected = checked.filter((c) => !c.ok);

  if (supported.length === 0) {
    return { status: "not-enough-evidence" as const, rejected };
  }

  return {
    status: rejected.length === 0 ? ("grounded" as const) : ("partial" as const),
    claims: supported.map((c) => ({
      text: c.claim.text,
      source: c.source, // id + updatedAt travel with the claim
    })),
    rejected, // review queue: never rendered as an answer
  };
}
```

What the refactor buys and what it does not. Empty citations can no longer pass, because there is no `every` over an empty list and `supported.length === 0` short-circuits to abstention. A fabricated quote now fails, because the quote is compared with the cited chunk's normalized text. The rendered answer is assembled from supported claims only, with source id and date attached, and rejected claims go to review instead of to the user.

It does not prove entailment. A verbatim quote can be real and still be misread: "refunds are available within 30 days" quoted from a clause about Enterprise accounts passes the lexical check and answers the wrong question. So the last step is routing, not more string comparison: for high-impact claims — money, deadlines, eligibility, security — resolve the answer against a deterministic policy lookup, and send anything the check cannot settle to human review. Then measure the thing you actually promised: on a labelled set, score the fraction of atomic claims supported by the cited source (the FActScore approach), break it down by claim type, and gate prompt, retrieval and model changes on that number. No single mitigation removes hallucination; grounding, abstention, verification and measurement are what move it.

Sources: Anthropic, reduce hallucinations (https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations); FActScore, Min et al. (https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14251).
